# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_channel_create(uint32_t options, vx_handle out[2]);
vx_status vx_channel_write(vx_handle ch, const void *bytes, uint32_t len,
                           const vx_handle *handles, uint32_t count);
vx_status vx_channel_read(vx_handle ch, void *bytes, uint32_t cap,
                          vx_handle *handles, uint32_t count_cap,
                          vx_msg_size *actual);
vx_status vx_channel_call(vx_handle ch, vx_call *args, vx_instant deadline);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A channel is a pair of ends, and the control plane of the system: bootstrap, passing handles, and rare control requests (see the kernel's design, 01 §4.2).
Bulk data goes through rings instead.
A message written to one end is queued at the other.
It is a datagram, not part of a byte stream: a body of 16 to 65536 bytes, `VX_CHANNEL_MAX_BYTES`, that starts with a header, and up to 64 handles, `VX_CHANNEL_MAX_HANDLES`.

```c
typedef struct vx_msg_header {
  uint32_t txid;    // matches a reply to its call;
                    // 0 for a message that wants none
  uint32_t ordinal; // the protocol's operation
  uint32_t flags;
  uint32_t sender_intent; // enum vx_intent of the sending thread
} vx_msg_header;

typedef struct vx_msg_size { // what channel_read and channel_call report
  uint32_t bytes;
  uint32_t handles;
} vx_msg_size;
```

The kernel writes <sender_intent> in every message it carries, so a reader can trust it; <ordinal> and <flags> are the protocol's.
The body is copied twice, in and out; the handles move, leaving the writer's table and arriving in the reader's with the rights they had.

`vx_channel_create` makes a channel and stores handles to its two ends in <out>.
<options> must be 0.
Each handle carries `VX_RIGHT_READ`, `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, `VX_RIGHT_WAIT`, `VX_RIGHT_SIGNAL`, `VX_RIGHT_DUPLICATE`, `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` and `VX_RIGHT_INSPECT`.
An end is destroyed with its last handle; the other end then sees its peer closed.

`vx_channel_write` sends the <len> bytes at <bytes> and the <count> handles in <handles> to the other end of <ch>.
<ch> needs `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, and each handle sent `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER`; a handle may appear once, and neither end of the channel may travel through it.
The handles are checked all together before any moves: if one is refused, or the length is wrong, nothing is sent and every handle stays.
Once the message is built the handles have left the caller's table, and a write that fails after that closes them.
Each end's queue holds at most 64 messages and 1 MiB; a write to a full queue fails with `VX_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`, and a program waits for room as it sees fit.
A message whose <txid> matches a `vx_channel_call` waiting at the other end is that call's reply: it goes straight to the caller, never through the queue.

`vx_channel_read` takes the next message queued at <ch>, copying its body to <bytes> and storing its handles, as new handles in the caller's table, in <handles>.
It never blocks: to wait for a message, bind the end to a port with `VX_TRIGGER_READABLE` (see port(2)).
If the message is larger than <cap> bytes or carries more than <count_cap> handles, it stays queued and the call fails with `VX_ERR_TOO_SMALL`.
Either way <*actual> is set to the message's size.
Messages queued before the other end closed are still read in order; only then does the call report `VX_ERR_PEER_CLOSED`.
<ch> needs `VX_RIGHT_READ`.

`vx_channel_call` writes a request and waits for its reply in one call, as a client does to a server:

```c
typedef struct vx_call {
  const void *wr_bytes;
  const vx_handle *wr_handles;
  void *rd_bytes;
  vx_handle *rd_handles;
  uint32_t wr_len, wr_count;
  uint32_t rd_cap, rd_count_cap;
  vx_msg_size actual;
  uint64_t lent;
} vx_call;
```

The request is <wr_len> bytes at <wr_bytes> and <wr_count> handles at <wr_handles>, checked and moved as `vx_channel_write`'s are.
The kernel picks the request's <txid>, a fresh value with the top bit set, and writes it in the header in place of the caller's.
The server replies by writing a message with the same <txid> to its end; the reply's body goes to <rd_bytes>, its handles to <rd_handles>, and its size to <actual>.
<ch> needs `VX_RIGHT_READ` and `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`.

Bit <i> of <lent> lends <wr_handles>[<i>] for the length of the call (01 §4.5, ADR-0043): it must be a VMO handle, which the caller keeps, and the server is sent a lease of it instead (vmo(2)), with that handle's rights but `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`.
When the call returns, however it ends, by the reply, its deadline, an interrupt, the server's end closing or the caller being killed, the kernel revokes the lease: the server's mappings of it lose their pages, a touch raises `VX_EXCEPTION_REVOKED`, and every use of it answers `VX_ERR_REVOKED`.
So a host that calls a plugin with a deadline gets its memory back however the plugin fails, and keeps no list of what to revoke.
A lent handle that is not a VMO fails the call with `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`, a lease or memory that cannot be leased with `VX_ERR_INVALID` or `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`, and a bit past <wr_count> with `VX_ERR_INVALID`, before anything is sent.

While it waits, the caller lends its scheduling, its intent and its scheduling context's budget, to the server (sched(2), M6 step 6d6c2): to the thread its request wakes from a port wait, then to the thread that reads it, which keeps the loan until the reply, and until it next blocks after it; a thread runs on a loan only if it is above its own. Each switches straight to the other: the woken server runs on the caller's CPU, the answered caller on the replier's.

A call that ends without its reply, by its <deadline>, an interrupt or a kill, takes back its request if the server has not read it yet: the server never sees it, and its handles are closed.
An interrupted call whose request the server has read goes on waiting, until the reply or <deadline>, so no answer is lost; the interrupt is delivered when it returns.
A server that holds a call answers it before it interrupts the caller.

# EXAMPLES

From the kernel's tests, a client:

```c
    request rq = {.n = n}, reply = {};
    vx_call call = {.wr_bytes = &rq, .wr_len = sizeof rq, .rd_bytes = &reply,
                    .rd_cap = sizeof reply};
    CHECK(vx_channel_call(ch[0], &call, after_ms(2000)) == VX_OK);
```

and its server, which waits on a port and answers each request with its own header, and so its <txid>:

```c
  for (int served = 0; served < 2;) {
    vx_packet pk;
    vx_port_bind(port, s->server_end, VX_TRIGGER_READABLE, 1, 0);
    if (vx_port_wait(port, after_ms(2000), 0, &pk, 1) != 1) break;
    request rq;
    vx_msg_size size;
    while (vx_channel_read(s->server_end, &rq, sizeof rq, nullptr, 0,
                           &size) == VX_OK) {
      rq.n *= 2;
      vx_channel_write(s->server_end, &rq, sizeof rq, nullptr, 0);
      served++;
    }
  }
```

